{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices, especially in downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path (roughly 6–12 €/t on average in these examples).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market rolls over, the model keeps pricing near the prior regime for ~1–2 weeks. Add a stronger short-horizon momentum term (or explicitly detect break-of-trend) to pull the first 10–15 days down faster.",
    "Insufficient mean reversion after highs: following elevated recent history (low-80s), the model projects stabilization/rebound, but outcomes mean-revert sharply into low-70s/high-60s. Enforce stronger mean reversion proportional to how far spot is above a 20–30d moving average.",
    "Overshoot on rebound scenarios: the model frequently predicts rebounds back to ~82–86 even while realized stays ~68–73. Cap upside projections during drawdowns (e.g., limit max 30d-ahead price to recent 10d high minus a drawdown-dependent haircut).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: bias grows with horizon (later days are even more overstated). Use a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (small for days 1–5, larger for days 15–30).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast paths are too smooth and too high; realized declines are steeper. Increase downside sensitivity (asymmetric error correction: larger adjustment when recent returns are negative)."
  ]
}
